In addition to generating reducers this way, you might also want to generate action creators using the same approach, and could generate them both at the same time with helper functions. See [Action/Reducer Generators](https://github.com/markerikson/redux-ecosystem-links/blob/master/action-reducer-generators.md) and [Reducers](https://github.com/markerikson/redux-ecosystem-links/blob/master/reducers.md) libraries for action/reducer utilities.

## Collection / Item Reducer Pattern

While higher-order reducers are most commonly used to specialize a single reducer for a specific piece of state, they can also be used to manage _collections_ of similar state slices. The "collection / item reducer pattern" allows you to reuse the same reducer logic across multiple instances of state, updating only the specific item that an action targets based on an additional parameter (such as an index or a name) included in the action object.

This can be particularly useful if you need to manage many instances of the same type of data and the number of instances is not known ahead of time, since it is difficult (or impossible) to set up a fixed set of slice reducers in a `combineReducers` call. In general, though, this pattern should only be used if you are confident that the range of possible collection sizes is small, or if the data is not going to be nested as deeply.

Here's a typical set up, with a basic `counter` reducer that handles `INCREMENT` and `DECREMENT` actions:

```js
function counter(state = 0, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'INCREMENT':
      return state + 1
    case 'DECREMENT':
      return state - 1
    default:
      return state
  }
}
```

We can use this reducer with an array, where each item in the collection is updated by targeting its `position` in the array. Every time an action is dispatched, we use the `index` property included in the action to figure out which item in the array should be updated, and then use array spread and slicing to produce a new array with the updated item in place:

```js
function counters(state = [], action) {
  const { index } = action

  if (index === undefined) {
    return state
  }

  switch (action.type) {
    case 'INCREMENT':
      return [
        ...state.slice(0, index),
        ...[(state[index] || 0) + 1],
        ...state.slice(index + 1)
      ]
    case 'DECREMENT':
      return [
        ...state.slice(0, index),
        ...[(state[index] || 0) - 1],
        ...state.slice(index + 1)
      ]
    default:
      return state
  }
}
```

For an object/map-based collection, we can instead include a `name` property in the action to identify which piece of state we want to update. The reducer for the collection can then use object spread and computed property names to generate a new object with only the specified key updated, leaving all other entries untouched:

```js
const initialState = {
  A: 0,
  B: 0,
  C: 0
}

function counters(state = initialState, action) {
  const { key } = action

  if (key === undefined) {
    return state
  }

  switch (action.type) {
    case 'INCREMENT':
      return {
        ...state,
        [key]: state[key] + 1
      }
    case 'DECREMENT':
      return {
        ...state,
        [key]: state[key] - 1
      }
    default:
      return state
  }
}
```

Note that this approach is effectively equivalent to using higher-order reducers like `createCounterWithNameData` from above to generate a slice reducer for each item in the collection, but without requiring a fixed set of slice reducers to be set up in advance via `combineReducers`. Instead, a single reducer manages the entire collection, and the "which item to update" decision is made purely from the information included in each action.

